cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  pointmodel: decomposing a pointing error budget with an explicit statement of
  what the observed geometry cannot separate
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-01
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Fits the classical nine-term alt-azimuth pointing model -- azimuth and
  elevation encoder index, collimation, two azimuth-axis tilt components, axis
  non-perpendicularity, two gravitational flexure terms and an atmospheric
  refraction term -- to measured cross-elevation and elevation residuals by
  weighted least squares, with a full covariance, a confidence interval on
  every term, a condition number and a per-term variance inflation factor. The
  headline capability is a refusal. Over a restricted observing geometry
  standard terms become mutually unidentifiable and least squares returns one
  specific split anyway; this package performs a rank analysis on the singular
  value decomposition of the weighted design matrix, names the near-null
  directions in terms of the physical coefficients, refuses to report a
  per-term split along them, and reports the identifiable linear combinations
  instead. There is deliberately no learned component: the problem is a linear
  fit whose entire difficulty is conditioning, and a model fitted here would
  predict a confident split exactly where no split exists. Validation is level
  2. Measured on a 43 to 47 degree elevation band with 300 observations and 2
  arcsec residual noise, the condition number is 6.49e5, seven of the nine
  terms are refused, the post-fit residual is 1.91 arcsec against the declared
  2.0 and the elevation index is wrong by 55,786 arcsec; on a single-azimuth
  sweep the least-squares split of the azimuth-axis tilt is repeatable across
  200 noise realisations to 2.32 arcsec and biased by 16.125, while the
  textbook normal-equation solver reports formal standard errors 294,000 times
  larger than the corresponding scatter. The design matrix is checked against an exact
  composition of rotation matrices using a bound from the operator-norm Taylor
  remainder of a product of matrix exponentials, which shares no mathematics
  with it; the measured worst error-to-bound ratio is between 0.2627 and
  0.3888 across five decades of misalignment amplitude. Refraction is
  cross-checked against a numerical integration of the refraction integral
  through a standard-atmosphere profile written in this repository; the
  normalised shape the model uses agrees to 1.96 per cent over 10 to 85
  degrees, and the absolute refraction agrees to 4.1270 arcsec with an optical
  refractivity constant, inside the 4.2 arcsec quoted for the empirical
  formula, but to only 8.4736 arcsec with a dry-air radio constant, which
  exceeds it and is reported as measured. Robust Huber and RANSAC fits run
  alongside least squares with their disagreement reported as a diagnostic and
  neither preferred; RANSAC is measured to collapse between 10 and 20 per cent
  acquisition-failure contamination at a point its own subset-purity
  probability predicts. Eighty-three negative controls were run across four
  scripts, 61 of which were declared must-fire and fired and 22 of which were
  declared must-not-fire and did not.
  All numbers are measured against synthetic residual sets generated by the
  package's own exact rotation model; no measurement of any real instrument
  appears anywhere. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not
  approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - pointing-model
  - telescope
  - antenna
  - alt-azimuth
  - least-squares
  - identifiability
  - atmospheric-refraction
  - robust-regression
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ulich
        given-names: "B. L."
    title: >-
      Millimeter-wave radio telescopes: gain and pointing characteristics
    journal: "International Journal of Infrared and Millimeter Waves"
    volume: 2
    issue: 2
    year: 1981
    start: 293
    end: 310
    notes: >-
      The pointing-error term set for a large alt-azimuth antenna: encoder
      index errors, collimation, azimuth-axis tilt and axis
      non-perpendicularity. This package takes the LIST of terms from the
      published literature and derives each column's functional form and sign
      itself, in pointmodel.terms, because sign conventions differ between
      implementations.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Wallace
        given-names: "P. T."
    title: "A rigorous algorithm for telescope pointing"
    collection-title: "Proceedings of SPIE"
    volume: 4848
    year: 2002
    start: 125
    end: 136
    notes: >-
      The published description of the geometric pointing model whose term
      names IA, IE, CA, AN, AW, NPAE and TF are used here because they are
      what a pointing engineer will recognise. The signs in this package are
      derived, not taken from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Mangum
        given-names: "J. G."
      - family-names: Wallace
        given-names: "P."
    title: >-
      Atmospheric refractive electromagnetic wave bending and propagation
      delay
    journal: "Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific"
    volume: 127
    issue: 947
    year: 2015
    notes: >-
      The place of a refraction term in a pointing model, and the statement
      that its coefficient is fitted rather than assumed, which is why the RF
      column here is a normalised shape and the scale is a fitted parameter.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Meeus
        given-names: "J."
    title: "Astronomical Algorithms"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Willmann-Bell"
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      Chapter 16, "Atmospheric Refraction". Equation (16.4) is Bennett's
      formula, the refraction model used here, and the chapter states its
      accuracy as about 0.07 arcminutes and gives the pressure and temperature
      correction factor. That 0.07 arcminute figure is the acceptance
      criterion of this package's refraction cross-check and is published, not
      chosen here.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bennett
        given-names: "G. G."
    title: "The calculation of astronomical refraction in marine navigation"
    journal: "Journal of Navigation"
    volume: 35
    issue: 2
    year: 1982
    start: 255
    end: 259
    notes: >-
      The original publication of the refraction formula reproduced by Meeus
      as equation (16.4).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Smith
        given-names: "E. K."
      - family-names: Weintraub
        given-names: "S."
    title: >-
      The constants in the equation for atmospheric refractive index at radio
      frequencies
    journal: "Proceedings of the IRE"
    volume: 41
    issue: 8
    year: 1953
    start: 1035
    end: 1037
    notes: >-
      The dry-air refractivity constant of 77.6 K/hPa used by this package's
      independent ray trace. Its 1.7508 per cent difference from the optical
      constant is the measured cause of the refraction cross-check's absolute
      disagreement.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ciddor
        given-names: "P. E."
    title: "Refractive index of air: new equations for the visible and near infrared"
    journal: "Applied Optics"
    volume: 35
    issue: 9
    year: 1996
    start: 1566
    end: 1573
    notes: >-
      The optical refractivity of standard air, from which this package
      back-computes its optical constant of 78.9586 K/hPa. The arithmetic is
      written out in pointmodel.refraction so that a reader can check it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Saastamoinen
        given-names: "J."
    title: >-
      Atmospheric correction for the troposphere and stratosphere in radio
      ranging of satellites
    collection-title: >-
      The Use of Artificial Satellites for Geodesy, Geophysical Monograph 15
    publisher:
      name: "American Geophysical Union"
    year: 1972
    start: 247
    end: 251
    notes: >-
      The standard treatment of the troposphere's effect on a radio ray,
      cited for the hydrostatic structure of the atmosphere the refraction
      integral is taken through. No numerical value in this repository is
      taken from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Auer
        given-names: "L. H."
      - family-names: Standish
        given-names: "E. M."
    title: >-
      Astronomical refraction: computational method for all zenith angles
    journal: "Astronomical Journal"
    volume: 119
    issue: 5
    year: 2000
    start: 2472
    end: 2474
    notes: >-
      The refraction integral and the substitutions needed at large zenith
      angles. Equation (4) of pointmodel.refraction is that integral; this
      package evaluates it only above 2 degrees elevation, where the
      substitutions are not needed.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Smart
        given-names: "W. M."
    title: "Textbook on Spherical Astronomy"
    edition: "6th"
    publisher:
      name: "Cambridge University Press"
    year: 1977
    notes: >-
      Chapter 3, the derivation of the refraction integral from Snell's
      invariant in a spherically symmetric atmosphere, and the
      plane-parallel limit that pins the sign and leading factor of this
      package's ray trace.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Huber
        given-names: "P. J."
    title: "Robust estimation of a location parameter"
    journal: "Annals of Mathematical Statistics"
    volume: 35
    issue: 1
    year: 1964
    start: 73
    end: 101
    notes: >-
      The Huber loss of equation (25). The tuning constant 1.345 for 95 per
      cent Gaussian efficiency and the asymptotic sandwich covariance of
      equation (27) are from the same author's "Robust Statistics" (Wiley,
      1981), chapter 7.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Fischler
        given-names: "M. A."
      - family-names: Bolles
        given-names: "R. C."
    title: >-
      Random sample consensus: a paradigm for model fitting with applications
      to image analysis and automated cartography
    journal: "Communications of the ACM"
    volume: 24
    issue: 6
    year: 1981
    start: 381
    end: 395
    notes: >-
      RANSAC. This package departs from the paper in two declared ways: the
      subset is not minimal, and subset fits go through the same SVD
      pseudo-inverse as everything else so that a degenerate subset yields a
      minimum-norm solution rather than an exception.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rousseeuw
        given-names: "P. J."
      - family-names: Croux
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Alternatives to the median absolute deviation"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 88
    issue: 424
    year: 1993
    start: 1273
    end: 1283
    notes: >-
      The 1.4826 consistency constant making a scaled median absolute
      deviation an unbiased estimator of the Gaussian standard deviation,
      used as the robust scale of equation (26).
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Golub
        given-names: "G. H."
      - family-names: "Van Loan"
        given-names: "C. F."
    title: "Matrix Computations"
    edition: "4th"
    publisher:
      name: "Johns Hopkins University Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Chapters 2 and 5 for the singular value decomposition, the numerical
      rank and the conditioning of a least squares problem, and for the
      statement that forming the normal equations squares the condition
      number. The last of those is why pointmodel.wls.fit does not use them
      and why pointmodel.wls.normal_equation_fit exists only to demonstrate
      the failure.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Bevington
        given-names: "P. R."
      - family-names: Robinson
        given-names: "D. K."
    title: "Data Reduction and Error Analysis for the Physical Sciences"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "McGraw-Hill"
    year: 2003
    notes: >-
      Chapter 7, weighted least squares and the covariance of the estimate,
      equations (19) and (20) of pointmodel.wls.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      U.S. Standard Atmosphere, 1976
    institution:
      name: "NOAA, NASA and the United States Air Force"
    year: 1976
    notes: >-
      The troposphere lapse rate of 6.5 K/km, the 11 km tropopause and the
      hydrostatic pressure profile through which this package's refraction
      integral is taken.
